<CHANGES><![CDATA[
## v2026.11.00

### Added

- **Dracula style (#590):** Settings > Appearance now offers Dracula, which keeps the Default fonts and layout and recolours every page, chart, badge, dialog and the Logs console. Dracula is always dark, so the colour mode buttons are disabled while it's selected, and your saved mode comes back when you choose another style. Text meets WCAG AA, and a few official colours are lightened where they would not. Palette by Dracula Theme (<https://draculatheme.com>), MIT licence.
- **Catppuccin style (#591):** Settings > Appearance now offers Catppuccin, with Latte in light mode and Mocha in dark mode, and System follows your device. It keeps the Default fonts and layout. Latte's accents are darkened where the official colours are too light to read on its pale backgrounds. Palette by Catppuccin (<https://catppuccin.com>), MIT licence.

### Changed

- Configure engineering agent skills with GitHub issue tracking, canonical triage labels, and glossary/ADR guidance in `docs/agents/`.
- **Clearer navigation names (#531):** The Recovery page is now called Disaster Recovery in the sidebar, the More sheet, its heading and its tab title, so it no longer reads like Restore or Recover Vault. Replication moves from Recover to Protect and gets its own two-way arrows icon instead of sharing Restore's. Links and bookmarks such as `#/recovery` and `#/replication` are unchanged, and replica mode still shows Replication and hides Disaster Recovery.
- **Ansible connects with SSH keys only (#571):** Deploy, verify, uninstall and replica runs now need `ansible_ssh_private_key_file` in `ansible/inventory.yml` and stop with a clear error if the key is missing or a password is still set. `sshpass` and temporary password files are gone, and new host keys are accepted once and then checked. See `ansible/README.md` to move an existing inventory.
- **Log warnings show their cause (#534):** With Details off, warning and error rows now show the affected item, status and error, such as "Health check · plex · unhealthy · Timed out", and warnings about different containers or statuses no longer fold together. Any row with details can be opened inline by mouse, keyboard or touch, and a folded row lists each entry; opening a row pauses auto-follow so it stays put. A new Problems filter shows only warnings and errors, the jump button now reads "Oldest", level labels and counts are larger, and row copy works by keyboard and touch. Rows are slightly taller to make room for 24 px controls. Copy and export are unchanged.
- **One clear backup status (#527):** The Dashboard now leads with a banner that gives one verdict, names each problem and links to the page that fixes it, and it won't say "healthy" while items are unprotected, data is loading or a request failed. The health tile's text wraps instead of truncating and is qualified the same way, so a score of 100 with 3 of 19 items unprotected reads "Backups OK · 3 unprotected". The progress tile only shows while a run is active; saved layouts and Customize still work. The 3-2-1 panel now reads "Configured" instead of "Met" while no items are backed up, because the rule only checks your configuration.
- **Embedded Backups view fits Unraid pages (#526):** With the top-navigation link on, Vault now shows a compact row of links instead of its own header, sidebar and bottom bar, the frame sizes to its content so the page scrolls as one, and dialogs stay on screen. It starts in Unraid's light or dark theme unless you've chosen a mode in Vault. The standalone view is unchanged.
- **Mobile navigation and touch targets (#530):** On phones, More now opens a sheet listing every page the bottom bar has no room for, and the hamburger menu is gone, so there is one way to get around. More shows as current on pages inside it. "More info" buttons and small actions now reach 24 px, with a 44 px hit area for info buttons and toast dismiss on touch screens. File tree checkboxes are larger. The Logs heading now sits above a toolbar that wraps, so nothing is clipped at 375 px.
- **Navigation and page semantics (#529):** Navigation items and Dashboard tiles are now real links, so they open in a new tab. Each page has its own tab title, and focus moves to the page heading after navigation. Settings tabs follow the ARIA tabs pattern with arrow, Home and End keys, navs and landmarks are named, the menu button reports its state, and the unlabelled Jobs, Restore and Settings fields now have names. The 3-2-1 panel's goal and dismiss buttons no longer sit inside its toggle.
- **Typed confirmation (#525):** Replacing the Vault database, restoring after failed pre-flight checks and purging all history now use an in-app dialog that names the source, states what will be lost and enables its confirm button only once you type `REPLACE`, `RESTORE` or `PURGE`, because these actions can't be undone. Cancel keeps initial focus. Normal restores keep their current review step.
- **Retro theme (#532):** The 8-Bit and 16-Bit themes are merged into one Retro theme with the same self-hosted pixel fonts. Settings now offers Default, 1-Bit and Retro, and saved 8-Bit and 16-Bit preferences move to Retro automatically.

### Fixed

- **Daemon shutdown waits for heartbeat writes (#620):** Vault now cancels and joins its heartbeat writer before returning from shutdown or a late startup failure, so the writer cannot recreate files after cleanup starts. The immediate first heartbeat is preserved.
- **Clearer screen reader names (#608):** Fields and headings with a "More info" button are read by their own name, such as "Max retries", and each button is named after its field, such as "More info: Max retries". Row buttons name their item, such as "Run backup now: Docker & Appdata", "Edit storage: Offsite S3" and "Dismiss: Backup size spike (Job #1)", and status buttons keep their state first, such as "Connected: Offsite S3". The Dashboard's Restore and Run Now buttons do the same. The storage form's staging and deduplication checkboxes, also in the Recover wizard, have short names and read their explanation as a description, which says why deduplication is locked when you edit a destination. Each Anomalies row is a heading, so you can jump between anomalies, and each field is read with its column name. In the job editor, the "More info" buttons beside each option section are no longer nested inside the section toggle, and the "Verify backups" and "Defer remote upload" switches have proper labels. An open tooltip is now part of the page's main content, and pressing Escape closes just the tooltip instead of the dialog it's in. In the 1-Bit style, the Recovery page's "Configure backup jobs" link has the same tighter letter spacing as the button it replaced. Nothing else looks different.
- **MCP restores no longer run alongside backups or other restores (#611):** `restore_item` didn't take the lock that backups, restores, garbage collection and orphan deletes share, so it could write to the same appdata, VM disk or folder as one of them. It now refuses straight away with "a backup, restore or maintenance task is running" while any of these is running, and holds them off until it finishes. Shutdown also waits for it. If an MCP restore's container has been replaced by the time it records Unraid's update status, the entry is left for the new container instead of being cleared.
- **Replication no longer keeps a lock for every source ID it is asked about (#610):** Updates and syncs of replication sources that don't exist now return not found without leaving anything behind, and a source's lock is released once nothing for it is running, so requests can no longer grow the daemon's memory without limit.
- **Embedded view scroll tidy-up (#598):** Restarting the embedded view no longer leaves earlier scroll listeners behind, and each scroll update in the embedded Logs console measures the page once instead of several times.
- **Screen readers hear which option is on (#529):** The Jobs status filter and the Restore type filter now report which choice is selected, Restore item cards report whether they are selected, and the Restore steps mark the current step and keep their names on phones, where they used to read as "1", "2" and "3". Each job's on/off switch is named "Enable" plus the job's name, so "on" means enabled. A History run with details is now a proper expand button that reports whether it's open, and its anomaly badge is no longer inside it. "Recover Vault", "Configure backup jobs" and the recovery wizard's "Go to Restore" and "Go to Dashboard" are now links, so they open in a new tab. Every page is checked with an accessibility audit and a full keyboard pass, standalone and embedded, at desktop and phone widths.
- **No false error when a deleted restore point's files are already gone (#603):** Deleting a restore point right after its job, or after another cleanup removed its files, logged "Failed to delete backup files … files may remain on storage" although nothing was left. Files and folders that are already gone now count as deleted on every storage type, and real delete failures still log an error.
- **MCP restores that need approval say so (#601):** `restore_item` used to answer "restore started" even when the restore was then refused for needing approval, and the refusal only reached the log. It now waits until the restore is past its approval checks and returns the refusal as an error that says to approve it in the Vault UI under Restore → Container approvals. MCP still can't approve a restore. A deduplicated container restore can take as long as the image pull to answer, and a classic one from an imported or replicated backup as long as the download. An MCP restore is still not a tracked run; `docs/mcp.md` explains where its outcome shows up.
- **A failed deduplicated container restore puts the image tag back (#599):** If the restore failed after pointing the tag at the backed-up image, the tag stayed there, so a later pull or recreation using the tag got the older image. The tag now goes back to the image it named before, unless someone else moved it during the restore. A tag that didn't exist before the restore stays on the backed-up image, and a restore that can't tell what the tag names stops before changing anything.
- **Unraid's update status matches the restored container (#599):** After a deduplicated restore that used the image's current tag, Unraid's Docker page could compare the backup-time digest with the registry, and "Check for Updates" kept it because Unraid only reads the image's digest when none is recorded. Vault now records the digest of the image the restored container was created from, read from the container rather than the tag, or clears the entry so Unraid reads it, and only once the restore has succeeded.
- **Anomalies no longer go missing between pages (#587):** With many open anomalies, ones detected in the same second as a page boundary could be skipped, on the Anomalies page and for clients that poll instead of using live updates. The Anomalies page now lists newest-detected first and pages in that order, so one seen again while you page through stays where it is. The Dashboard card and the MCP `list_anomalies` tool still show the most recently seen first. `GET /anomalies` returns `next_cursor` only with `order=id`. A browser tab left open across the upgrade keeps working; reload it to load more.
- **Polling clients keep reporting resolved anomalies (#593):** When looking up a resolved anomaly kept failing, it no longer holds up the ones behind it, and the waiting list is capped at 200, past which the Anomalies page reloads instead.
- **Replicated backups keep their outcome on busy peers (#600):** A backup whose run is older than the peer's newest 1,000 runs is now looked up on its own, so one that skipped files stays partial on the replica instead of showing as a success. If the peer can't report that run, or its history fails to load for any reason other than the peer not having it, the copy is marked partial and its log says the outcome is unconfirmed. Peers serve single runs at the new `GET /api/v1/runs/{runId}`.
- **A replicated job's destination follows its source (#600):** Editing a replicated job through the jobs API or MCP can no longer move it to another storage destination, which left restore and pre-flight checks looking in the wrong place. Changing a replication source's destination now moves its replicated jobs with it, and is refused with a clear message while any of them has restore points on the old destination; delete those jobs or create a new source instead. It is also refused, with a conflict error, while a sync of that source is running, and a backup whose job moved during its copy is not recorded. Other source edits, such as the schedule, are never blocked.
- **Peer clocks can't push backups past retention (#600):** A replicated backup dated more than 24 hours ahead of this server's clock is recorded at the time it was copied, and its run log says why, so it can't outlast newer backups.
- **Replication's destination choice survives a lost working database (#600):** When a sync records the destination it chose, the database is flushed to flash, as for other configuration changes.
- **Replication records the backups it copies (#585):** Until now, replication downloaded a peer's backup files but never recorded them, so replicated jobs had no restore points and nothing could be restored from the replica, while the sync still reported success. Each copied backup is now a restore point of the replicated job and appears in History, dated when the peer took it and marked Partial when the peer's backup was partial, for example when it skipped files. Incremental and differential backups stay linked to the backup they build on, so restoring one includes the unchanged files. A source with no storage destination set now saves the destination its first sync picks, and replicated jobs left without a destination are bound to the one the source uses. Deduplicated backups can't be replicated yet, because their shared data store isn't copied. A sync now reports them as failed with that reason and downloads nothing for them, instead of recording copies that couldn't be restored. VM and ZFS backups on a deduplicated destination are stored the classic way and still replicate. The first sync after upgrading downloads those backups again, because the earlier copies were never recorded. A backup that can't be downloaded or recorded now makes the sync Partial or Failed, logs a warning, and is retried on the next sync. Large file downloads are no longer cut off after 10 minutes. They stop only if the peer sends nothing for 2 minutes, and slow local storage doesn't count as the peer stalling.
- **Replication errors give the reason (#578):** A refused connection now shows the peer's own short message, such as how to create an API key, instead of only "status 401".
- **Build after the mDNS library update (#588):** The main module now matches the bundled library's `netlink` update, so Vault builds again.
- **Retro fonts load in the plugin (#532):** The pixel fonts returned 404 when the app was served from the Unraid plugin path, so Retro fell back to system fonts.
- **Jobs bulk bar on phones (#530):** The bulk action bar now wraps inside a 375 px screen and sits above the mobile navigation, standalone and embedded, with full-size touch targets.
- **Embedded Logs scroll with the page (#526):** In the embedded view, the Logs console no longer has a scroll area of its own: the Unraid page scrolls through it like every other page, on desktop and phones. Auto-follow, Oldest, Jump to present, pausing when a row is opened, copy and export work as before, and their buttons stay on screen. The standalone view is unchanged.
- **Embedded skip link (#526):** In the embedded view, the skip link now scrolls the parent page so the heading it focuses is on screen.
- **Logs 404s:** The Logs page no longer requests run logs for runs that have been deleted, which filled the console with failed requests on every load. `/activity` now flags those entries with `run_gone`.
- **Log folding and empty states (#534):** Warnings with different causes no longer fold into one row. "No problems" now waits for older history to load, and says so if older history could not be loaded, with a retry. Each row's expand button names its item and time. Container health messages are redacted like storage errors.
- **Destructive confirmations (#525):** The recovery wizard now asks you to type REPLACE before replacing the database of a Vault that already has jobs or storage, or when it could not check; first-time recovery on an empty Vault keeps its light confirmation. Bulk job delete now asks first and names how many jobs it removes.
- **False "Backups healthy" (#527):** The Dashboard now treats skipped and cancelled runs, unhealthy storage, jobs overdue for a backup (including disabled ones) and a health score below 80 as needing attention, with a link for each, across every job rather than a ten-job sample. The health tile uses the same verdict, so "Backups mostly healthy" now reads "Needs attention". `/health/summary` adds `recent_partial`, `recent_skipped`, `recent_cancelled` and `stale_jobs`; skipped and cancelled runs lower the score, and a restore no longer counts as a last successful backup.
- **Accessible dialogs (#528):** Every dialog, the command palette and the file browsers now share one native `<dialog>`. Focus moves in on open, stays inside, and returns to the opener on close, and Escape closes only the topmost dialog. Restore, Recovery and New Job wizard steps move focus to the step heading. Toasts and tooltips stay usable above a dialog.
- **Retro and 1-Bit contrast (#532):** Text in both themes now meets WCAG AA in light and dark on every page, including replica mode and running restores, checked by the axe audit in the browser tests. Read-only replica settings are no longer dimmed below AA.
- **Contrast (#524):** Default light and dark text, navigation, status colours and primary buttons now meet WCAG 2.2 AA on every page, checked by an axe audit in the browser tests. Toasts gain a status icon and an opaque background. Retro palettes are unchanged.

### Removed

- **Legacy PHP pages (#533):** Removed the unlinked `VaultJobs`, `VaultStorage`, `VaultHistory`, `VaultRestore` and `VaultSettings` pages and their CSS and JavaScript. Bookmarks to the old `/Settings/VaultJobs`, `/Settings/VaultStorage`, `/Settings/VaultHistory`, `/Settings/VaultRestore` and `/Settings/VaultSettings` URLs stop working; use Settings → Utilities → Vault or the optional Backups navigation entry instead. Plugin upgrades delete these files from existing installs.

### Security

- **Web build tooling alerts (#615):** The web toolchain now pins minimatch 11.0.4 or later, which fixes a denial-of-service bug (GHSA-g3hv-75wg-m249) in the version ESLint pulled in. The deepmerge alert (GHSA-gcqx-w48m-w2h4) has no patched release; vite-plugin-svelte only uses it to merge the project's own build config, so it's accepted until a fix ships. Both are build-time only, so nothing in the shipped plugin changes.
- **Deduplicated plugin backups refuse a planted installer (#595):** The deduplicated plugin backup now reads the `.plg` installer the same way the classic backup does, so a symlink, FIFO or directory in its place fails the backup instead of copying another file into it. A missing installer is still optional. On Unraid `/boot` can't hold symlinks, so this mainly matters for other setups.
- **Startup only restores snapshots this server signed (#542):** Each database snapshot now gets a signature made with `vault.key`, and once a server has signed one, a snapshot that was replaced, forged or stripped of its signature is skipped at boot instead of replacing your settings and API key. Existing unsigned snapshots still restore until the first signed one is written; `ALLOW_UNSIGNED_SNAPSHOT="yes"` in `vault.cfg` restores an older one after that. Replacing the database from a storage backup now keeps the current API key.
- **Deduplicated container restores use the backed-up image (#542):** A restore pulls the image by the digest recorded at backup time and points the tag back at it, so a tag that moved since then no longer runs different code. If the digest is missing or unavailable, the restore stops before changing anything; tick _Use the image's current tag_ (API `allow_unpinned_image`) to go ahead.
- **Restore ignores files the backup never recorded (#542):** When a restore point has checksums, files on storage it did not record are skipped and named in the run log, so a planted `volume_0.tar.gz` can no longer overwrite the verified `volume_0.tar`. Two files that would restore to the same name now stop the restore.
- **Chain prune trusts only checked file lists (#542):** A container chain restore deletes resurrected files only when every step's file lists were checked against checksums this server recorded. Otherwise it keeps them and says so in the run log.
- **Imported containers need approval for host access (#547):** Restoring a container from an imported or replicated backup now stops if it asks for privileged mode, host networking, IPC or PID, added capabilities, devices or host paths outside `/mnt`, and lists them. Tick _Allow host-level access_ (API `allow_host_privileges`) to approve; MCP `restore_item` cannot approve and points to the UI instead. Restoring a privileged container from a backup this server made works as before, whether or not it was verified after writing.
- **ZFS receive keeps mount settings local (#547):** A ZFS restore no longer takes `mountpoint`, `canmount` or `overlay` from the stream. It keeps the values set on the existing dataset, otherwise inherits them, and mounts the dataset afterwards.
- **Bad data from peers and backup storage can no longer crash or exhaust the daemon (#559):** A replication peer sending null metadata no longer crashes Vault, and any panic during a sync now marks that sync failed. Replication JSON responses, run manifests, tar index sidecars and WebDAV chunk manifests have size limits well above real backups (1 GiB, 256 MiB, 4 GiB and 256 MiB). SFTP connects and handshakes time out after 60 seconds and NFS mount helpers after 3 minutes, so an unresponsive server no longer leaks a connection or process per health check. The MCP `test_storage` and file-listing tools now close their connections and NFS mounts.
- **SFTP connections always verify the host key (#551):** Vault no longer falls back to accepting any key. Destinations without a configured key keep working: Vault trusts the key seen on the first connection, records it in the activity log, and refuses a different one, with both fingerprints in the error. If you replace the server, enter the new key in the destination's new **Host Key Fingerprint** field. An unreadable `known_hosts_file` or malformed fingerprint is now an error. `vault recover` needs `host_key` or `known_hosts_file` and its error shows the fingerprint to check.
- **Retired server keys stop opening dedup destinations (#553):** After a successful key rewrap, Vault removes the old `_vault/repo.json.<time>.bak` copy once the new header is verified, and the next dedup backup removes copies left by earlier rewraps. Copies held in your storage provider's versioning or snapshots are outside Vault's reach.
- **Stronger backup passphrases (#553):** New passphrases need at least 12 characters and cannot be repetitive, a simple run, or only common words with digits and symbols. Existing passphrases keep working; change a weak one in Settings. Changing it does not re-encrypt existing backups.
- **Replication peers cannot be pointed at this host or cloud metadata (#567):** Replication refuses loopback, link-local, multicast and cloud metadata addresses at connection time, so DNS rebinding cannot get around it. LAN, Tailscale-style and public peers still work. Peer response bodies no longer appear in errors. Replicating through a local tunnel needs `VAULT_REPLICATION_ALLOW_LOOPBACK=1` in the daemon's environment, and HTTP proxy variables no longer apply to replication.
- **Plugin proxy stays on the local daemon (#567):** The Unraid page now ignores a `PORT` or `BIND_ADDRESS` in `vault.cfg` that is not valid for this host, and uses the defaults. Binding to one of the server's LAN addresses still works.
- **Terminal escapes in untrusted names are shown, not run (#568, #569):** `vault recover` and the bundled dnssd tools now show control characters from backup metadata or mDNS records as visible escapes.
- **Cleanup only trusts encrypted manifests it can verify (#550):** A file carrying the encrypted-manifest marker no longer lets a destination or job cleanup, or an import, treat its directory as Vault's. The manifest must decrypt and name that directory; if it can't, the directory stays and the orphan scan shows it. Recorded restore points are unaffected.
- **A replication peer can't reach another job's backups (#550, #555):** Paths overlapping another source's or local job's restore point, or inside `_vault`, are refused, and a job is reused only if this source created it. A sync whose local job name is taken, or whose job is on another destination, now fails with a clear error, and a sync with refused restore points reports the job as failed. Deletes also leave any path another job still holds, so overlaps left by older versions lose nothing.
- **ZFS restores ignore the dataset named in the backup (#550):** A forced receive now targets only the dataset configured on the job's item, or the dataset a locally created item is named after. Imported and replicated ZFS jobs, including jobs imported by older versions, need a dataset on the item or a dataset chosen as the restore destination.
- **Replicas block more destructive writes (#555):** Storage GC, orphan deletion, import, closing a breaker, deleting a destination's backup files, force-deleting a destination with dependent jobs, history and activity purges, anomaly acknowledgement and deleting a replication source now return 403, and reading stale items no longer writes. Creating, editing, testing and syncing sources remain available, as a replica needs them to receive backups.
- **Restores can't delete files through a tampered index (#555):** A chain restore's prune removes a path marked as a directory only if it is an empty directory.
- **GC stops rather than guess what is live (#562, #563):** Dedup GC deletes nothing if any restore point's manifest, nested volume manifest or metadata can't be read. This can hold back space until that restore point is fixed or deleted, but can't lose data.
- **Orphan cleanup can't race a backup (#562):** Deleting orphans now reports an error while a backup, restore, import or sync is running, and fails instead of proposing live files if restore points can't be read. GC, deleting a destination's files and job-directory sweeps also refuse to run during imports and syncs.
- **`vault dedup gc` and `repair` refuse to run alongside the service (#563):** Stop the service first, or use **Run cleanup** on the Storage page. The service won't start if it can't hold the lock that enforces this; `--no-maintenance-lock` overrides that for development runs.
- **Uninstall finds the snapshot on any pool (#562):** Uninstall used to look only on `/mnt/cache`, leaving the database snapshot behind on other pools.
- **Go 1.27.3 and updated dnssd dependencies (#536, #540, #541, #546):** Vault now builds with Go 1.27.3. The bundled mDNS library no longer declares an end-of-life Go version or vulnerable `golang.org/x/net`, and Dependabot and `make security-check` now cover it.
- **Release publishing is isolated from release code (#537):** Releases now run only from tags on `main`. The token that updates the plugin manifest on `main` no longer shares a job with code from the tag, and no longer appears in a git URL.
- **CI tool and token scope (#537):** CI now uses a fixed golangci-lint version, and the Codecov identity token is held by a job that runs no repository code.
- **Backup passphrases stay out of URLs (#560):** Scanning a destination for encrypted backups now sends the passphrase only in the request body, and `POST /api/v1/storage/{id}/scan` returns 400 if `passphrase` is in the query string. If an earlier version was used behind a reverse proxy, check its access logs for `/scan?...passphrase=`, purge those entries and rotate any passphrase found.
- **Credentials are encrypted at rest:** Storage passwords, S3 keys and replication API keys are now encrypted in the database with `vault.key`, and upgrading seals existing ones on first start, including local snapshots and flash copies. Same-server use and restores keep working. After restoring a database on a server with a different `vault.key`, affected destinations show **Credentials locked** until you restore that key or re-enter them, so keep a copy of `vault.key` off the server. See the disaster recovery guide.
- **Replication API keys stay hidden:** REST and MCP responses no longer return a source's API key, or storage secrets through MCP. Leave the key blank when editing to keep it. Changing a source's URL now requires entering the key again, and a redirect to another host no longer receives it. Changing where a storage destination connects also requires entering its secret again, and Test Connection while editing now uses the stored secret.
- **Diagnostics redaction:** Run logs, per-item settings, error text, activity details and URLs with embedded logins are now redacted in diagnostics bundles.
- **WebDAV URL logins are protected:** A username and password embedded in a WebDAV URL are no longer shown in responses or request URLs, and a WebDAV redirect to another host or from HTTPS to HTTP no longer receives the login.
- **Plain HTTP warnings:** The daemon log, plugin settings and Replication and WebDAV forms now warn when API keys or logins travel over plain HTTP to another machine. Existing LAN setups keep working. Setting only one of `--tls-cert` and `--tls-key` now stops the daemon instead of silently using plain HTTP.
- **Restores refuse planted FIFOs:** Restoring a file onto a FIFO or other non-regular file now fails instead of hanging or sending restored data to whoever reads it.
- **API key now required for replication and Home Assistant (#538):** Using Vault from the Unraid web UI still needs no key or password, on any bind address. Replication between Vault servers, and Home Assistant when Vault is bound to the server's LAN IP, now need an API key even if you never set one: generate it under Settings > Security > API Access, then add it to the other server or to Home Assistant. Headless servers and Docker replicas can use the new `vault api-key generate` command.
- **Web pages can no longer reach the API (#544, #552, #565):** A page on another site is refused on every method and on the WebSocket, including over localhost, and DNS rebinding no longer earns local trust. Cross-origin access is off by default; list exact origins with `--allowed-origin` or `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` in `vault.cfg`.
- **Secrets are only available from the server (#544):** Showing the API key or backup passphrase, downloading `vault.key`, and generating, rotating or revoking the API key now work only from the Unraid web UI or the server itself, even with a valid key.
- **API key guessing is throttled (#564):** Failed key checks are limited per address and in total, so a flood can't exhaust the CPU. Setting the backup passphrase is rate limited too.
- **Health check requires access (#570):** `/api/v1/health` now follows the same rules as the rest of the API, so it no longer shows filesystem paths to anonymous callers.
- **Restores stay in approved locations (#543):** A typed restore destination must now be under `/mnt`, a temp directory, or `/boot/config/plugins/<name>`; system trees and the rest of the flash drive are refused. Paths read from a backup get the same treatment: in-place container volumes must be under `/mnt`, a Docker named volume, a plugin config directory, or a bind the container on this host already has, and VM disks and NVRAM must be in their usual locations. Otherwise restore to a custom destination under `/mnt`.
- **Symlinks can no longer redirect root file operations (#543, #545):** Folder backups, restores, ownership changes, destination clearing, staging cleanup, database snapshots and uninstall now refuse symlinks that lead out of their approved location, and validate where they write. Folder items can no longer use protected system trees such as `/etc` or `/var/lib`, including existing jobs, and plugin item names must be a single folder name.
- **Settings and storage paths are validated (#543, #545, #554):** Newlines in the snapshot path are refused, `vault.cfg` is revalidated at startup, and path settings can only be changed through their own endpoints. Uninstall only deletes a custom snapshot if it really is a SQLite database. WebDAV chunk manifests and NFS base paths must stay inside their own object and export.

- **Storage health errors are redacted:** Credentials echoed in a storage health-check error are now removed before the message is saved, broadcast or logged.
]]></CHANGES>

<!-- Prepare persistent plugin storage and prune stale cached bundles -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
    <INLINE><![CDATA[
PLUGIN_DIR="/boot/config/plugins/vault"
CURRENT_BUNDLE="&bundle;"

mkdir -p "$PLUGIN_DIR"

find "$PLUGIN_DIR" -maxdepth 1 -type f \( -name 'vault-*.txz' -o -name 'vault-*.tgz' \) \
    ! -name "$CURRENT_BUNDLE" -delete 2>/dev/null || true
]]></INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- Require Unraid 7.0 or later -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
    <INLINE><![CDATA[
source /etc/unraid-version
if [[ "${version%%.*}" -lt 7 ]]; then
    echo "Vault requires Unraid 7.0 or later"
    exit 1
fi
]]></INLINE>
</FILE>

<!--
    Stop any running Vault daemon before upgrading the package.

    Without this, upgradepkg replaces the on-disk binary while the previous
    daemon is still running, the post-install "start" sees an existing PID
    and exits silently, and the user keeps running the OLD binary until they
    reboot the server (issue #72).

    Persistent state under /boot/config/plugins/vault (vault.cfg, vault.db,
    sealed credentials) is intentionally left untouched so user settings and
    configuration survive the upgrade.
-->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
    <INLINE><![CDATA[
if [ -x /etc/rc.d/rc.vault ]; then
    echo "Stopping Vault daemon before upgrade..."
    /etc/rc.d/rc.vault stop || echo "Warning: rc.vault stop reported a non-zero status; continuing"
fi

# Belt-and-braces: ensure no stray vault process is still holding the binary.
attempts=15
while pidof vault >/dev/null 2>&1 && [ "$attempts" -gt 0 ]; do
    sleep 1
    attempts=$((attempts - 1))
done

if pidof vault >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "Vault daemon did not exit gracefully; forcing shutdown..."
    killall -9 vault 2>/dev/null || true
    sleep 1
fi

# Stale pidfile would block the post-install start step.
rm -f /var/run/vault.pid
]]></INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- Download and install the bundle -->
<FILE Name="/boot/config/plugins/&name;/&bundle;" Run="upgradepkg --install-new">
    <URL>&bundleURL;</URL>
    <SHA256>&sha256;</SHA256>
</FILE>

<!-- Post-install: create default config and start daemon -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
    <INLINE><![CDATA[
# Create default config if missing
CONFIG="/boot/config/plugins/vault/vault.cfg"
if [ ! -f "$CONFIG" ]; then
    cat > "$CONFIG" <<'CFGEOF'
PORT=24085
BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
MDNS_ADVERTISE=yes
# SNAPSHOT_PATH – Custom snapshot location (set by the daemon; do not edit manually)
CFGEOF
fi

# Create data directory
mkdir -p /boot/config/plugins/vault

# Ensure permissions
chmod 755 /usr/local/sbin/vault
chmod 755 /etc/rc.d/rc.vault
# apply.sh is executed directly by /update.php via popen() after a settings
# save; without the exec bit the call fails with a silent "Permission denied"
# and the daemon never restarts onto the new bind address/port.
chmod 755 /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/vault/include/apply.sh
# emhttp event scripts (array started / stopping_svcs) must be executable or
# Unraid silently skips them — the graceful reboot flush depends on them (#241).
chmod 755 /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/vault/event/started \
          /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/vault/event/stopping_svcs 2>/dev/null || true

# Remove files left by the legacy PHP UI (#533). This is explicit so the result
# doesn't depend on upgradepkg, and it also covers files a manual or dev deploy
# placed outside the package. Never touch ui/: the package just installed it.
# BEGIN legacy-ui-cleanup
LEGACY_UI_DIR="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/vault"
if [ -n "$LEGACY_UI_DIR" ] && [ -d "$LEGACY_UI_DIR" ]; then
    rm -f "$LEGACY_UI_DIR/VaultJobs.page" \
          "$LEGACY_UI_DIR/VaultRestore.page" \
          "$LEGACY_UI_DIR/VaultStorage.page" \
          "$LEGACY_UI_DIR/VaultHistory.page" \
          "$LEGACY_UI_DIR/VaultSettings.page"
    rm -rf "$LEGACY_UI_DIR/assets"
fi
# END legacy-ui-cleanup

# Restart cleanly: stop any leftover daemon (e.g. from an aborted previous
# upgrade) before starting the new binary. This guarantees the user is
# running the just-installed version without requiring a server reboot.
/etc/rc.d/rc.vault stop 2>/dev/null || true
/etc/rc.d/rc.vault start

echo "Vault plugin installed successfully"
]]></INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- Removal script -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
    <INLINE><![CDATA[
DAEMON="/usr/local/sbin/vault"
CONFIG_DIR="/boot/config/plugins/vault"
DB="${CONFIG_DIR}/vault.db"

# Stop daemon
/etc/rc.d/rc.vault stop 2>/dev/null || true

# Wait for the daemon to finish any final snapshot flush before cleanup.
attempts=20
while pidof vault >/dev/null 2>&1 && [ "$attempts" -gt 0 ]; do
    sleep 1
    attempts=$((attempts - 1))
done

if pidof vault >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    killall -9 vault 2>/dev/null || true
    sleep 1
fi

# Remove installed package files
PACKAGE=""
for pkg in /var/log/packages/vault-*; do
    [ -e "$pkg" ] || continue
    PACKAGE="$(basename "$pkg")"
done

if [ -z "$PACKAGE" ]; then
    for pkg in /boot/config/plugins/vault/vault-*.txz /boot/config/plugins/vault/vault-*.tgz; do
        [ -e "$pkg" ] || continue
        case "$pkg" in
            *.txz) PACKAGE="$(basename "$pkg" .txz)" ;;
            *.tgz) PACKAGE="$(basename "$pkg" .tgz)" ;;
        esac
    done
fi

if [ -x "$DAEMON" ]; then
    "$DAEMON" cleanup-uninstall \
        --db="$DB" \
        --config-dir="$CONFIG_DIR" \
        --binary-path="$DAEMON" \
        --rc-script="/etc/rc.d/rc.vault" \
        --plugin-dir="/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/vault" \
        --log-path="/var/log/vault.log" \
        --pid-file="/var/run/vault.pid" \
        --hybrid-working-dir="/var/local/vault" \
        || echo "Vault uninstall cleanup reported a warning; continuing with package removal"
fi

if [ -n "$PACKAGE" ]; then
    removepkg "$PACKAGE" 2>/dev/null || true
fi

# Fallback cleanup if the binary was already missing.
rm -f "$DAEMON"
rm -f /etc/rc.d/rc.vault
rm -rf /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/vault
rm -f /var/log/vault.log
rm -f /var/run/vault.pid
rm -rf /boot/config/plugins/vault/*.txz
rm -rf /boot/config/plugins/vault/*.tgz
# Empty-only: never remove a FUSE mount root that still holds anything.
rmdir /mnt/vault-fuse /mnt/addons/vault-fuse 2>/dev/null || true

echo "Vault plugin removed"
]]></INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
